<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
  <title>Final Practice Exam</title>

<section>

    <p>
      Be sure to try each question before looking at the solutions.
    </p>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-practicefinalexam">
    <title>Questions</title> 

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>Consider the system of equations:</p>
    <md>
      <mrow> 3x_1 - 2x_2 + 3x_3 \mathstrut \amp = -2 </mrow>
      <mrow> -3x_1 + x_2 + 2x_3 \mathstrut \amp = 0 </mrow>
    </md>
    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li><p>Write this system as a matrix equation <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Using row reduction, find a parametric description of the intersection of these two planes.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <ol>
      <li><p>
    <me>
      \begin{bmatrix}
      3 \amp -2 \amp 3 \\
      -3 \amp 1 \amp 2
      \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix}
      x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3
      \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      -2 \\ 0
      \end{bmatrix}
    </me>
  </p>
</li>

    <li><p>Put the following augmented matrix in RREF: 
    <md>
      \begin{bmatrix}
      3 \amp -2 \amp 3 \amp -2 \\
      -3 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 0
      \end{bmatrix}
      \sim
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 0 \amp -7/3 \amp 2/3 \\
      0 \amp 1 \amp -5 \amp 2
      \end{bmatrix}
    </md>
  </p>
    <p>The solution to this system can be described parametrically by</p>

    <me>
      \mathbf{x} =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2/3 \\ 2 \\ 0
      \end{bmatrix}
      +
      x_3
      \begin{bmatrix}
      7/3 \\ 5 \\ 1
      \end{bmatrix}
    </me>
</li>
</ol>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>Determine weights to make a true statement below, or explain why this cannot be done.</p>

    <me>
      \underline{\hspace{8mm}}\begin{bmatrix}2\\-1\\4\end{bmatrix}
      +
      \underline{\hspace{8mm}}\begin{bmatrix}7\\-36\\29\end{bmatrix}
      +
      \underline{\hspace{8mm}}\begin{bmatrix}1\\6\\-1\end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}10\\21\\8\end{bmatrix}
    </me>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p>Form an augmented matrix and row reduce:</p>

    <md>
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2 \amp 7 \amp 1 \amp 10 \\
      -1 \amp -36 \amp 6 \amp 21 \\
      4 \amp 29 \amp -1 \amp 8
      \end{bmatrix}
      \sim
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 0 \amp 1.2 \amp 7.8 \\
      0 \amp 1 \amp -0.2 \amp -0.8 \\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
      \end{bmatrix}
    </md>

    <p>So we have</p>

    <me>
      7.8 \begin{bmatrix}2\\-1\\4\end{bmatrix}
      + -0.8 \begin{bmatrix}7\\-36\\29\end{bmatrix}
      + 0 \begin{bmatrix}1\\6\\-1\end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}10\\21\\8\end{bmatrix}
    </me>

    <p>by choosing the free variable <m>x_3 = 0</m>.</p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>Suppose that</p>

    <me>
      A =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \\
      2 \amp 4 \amp 6 \amp 8 \\
      1 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 5
      \end{bmatrix}
    </me>

    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li><p>Compute <m>rank(A)</m>. Explain how you calculated this.</p></li>
      <li><p><m>Nul(A)</m> is a subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^k</m> for <m>k =\underline{\hspace{8mm}}</m></p></li>
      <li><p>Compute the dimension of <m>Nul(A)</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Find a basis for <m>Col(A)</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Find a basis for <m>Col(A)^\perp</m>.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li><p>
    <md>
      A \sim
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
      0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
      \end{bmatrix}
    </md>

    so that rank(<m>A</m>) = 2 since there are 2 rows containing a pivot.</p>
  </li>
<li>
    <p><m>A</m> has 4 columns so that <m>Nul(A)</m> is a subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m>. That is <m>k = 4</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>
    <p>The dimension of <m>Nul(A)</m> = 4 - rank(<m>A</m>) = 4 - 2 = 2.</p>
</li>
<li>
    <p>The pivot columns from the original matrix <m>A</m> form a basis for <m>Col(A)</m>. That is the basis is given by</p>

    <me>
      \left\{
      \begin{bmatrix}1\\2\\1\end{bmatrix},
      \begin{bmatrix}2\\4\\3\end{bmatrix}
      \right\}
    </me>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p><m>Col(A)^\perp</m> is the null space of <m>A^T</m>.</p>
    <p>Since
    <m>
      A^T =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
      2 \amp 4 \amp 3 \\
      3 \amp 6 \amp 4 \\
      4 \amp 8 \amp 5
      \end{bmatrix}
    </m>

    we can solve <m>A^T\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{0}</m> for <m>\mathbf{y} \in \mathbb{R}^3</m>.  But since </p>

    <me>
      A^T \sim
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 0
      \end{bmatrix}
    </me>

    <p>we have one free variable and a basis for the set of solutions, which is precisely a basis for <m>Col(A)^\perp</m>, is given by</p>

    <me>
      \left\{
      \begin{bmatrix}-2\\1\\0\end{bmatrix}
      \right\}
    </me>
    <p>Is it easy to check that this vector is both perpendicular to each vector found in the basis for <m>Col(A)</m> and linearly independent if placed in a set with those vectors.  </p>
  </li>
</ol>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>Let</p>

    <me>
      H =
      \left\{
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2a - 4b - 3c \\
      4a - 6b \\
      9a - b + 7c
      \end{bmatrix}
      : a,b,c \text{ are real}
      \right\}
    </me>

    <p>Explain why <m>H</m> is a subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.</p>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <me>
      H = span \left\{
      \begin{bmatrix}1\\4\\9\end{bmatrix},
      \begin{bmatrix}-4\\-6\\-1\end{bmatrix},
      \begin{bmatrix}-3\\0\\7\end{bmatrix}
      \right\}
    </me>

    <p>so it is a subspace.</p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>Define a specific linear transformation <m>T</m> and use a determinant to find the area of the parallelogram with vertices <m>(0,0)</m>, <m>(4,1)</m>, <m>(2,4)</m>, and <m>(-2,3)</m>.</p>


    <image width="50%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="parallelogram-image-finalexam-review">
    <diagram dimensions="(300, 300)" margins="5">
  <coordinates bbox="(-2,-2,5,5)">
    <grid-axes hticks="(-2,5,1)" vticks="(-2,5,1)" hlabels="(-1,1,4)" vlabels="(-1,1,4)"/>
    <vector v="(4,1)" alignment="se" stroke="red">
    </vector>
    <vector v="(-2,3)" alignment="se" stroke="red">
    </vector>
    <definition>points=((0,0),(-2,3),(2,4),(4,1))</definition>
    <polygon points="points" stroke="blue" closed="yes" 
    fill="blue" opacity="0.5" />
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
  </prefigure>
</image>

  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p>Define a linear transformation <m>T</m> so that</p>

    <me>
      T(\mathbf{e_1}) = \begin{bmatrix}4\\1\end{bmatrix}, \quad
      T(\mathbf{e_2}) = \begin{bmatrix}-2\\3\end{bmatrix}
    </me>

    <p>Then</p>

    <me>
      T(\mathbf{x}) = A\mathbf{x} =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      4 \amp -2 \\
      1 \amp 3
      \end{bmatrix}
    </me>

    <p>The area of the parallelogram is then</p>

    <me>
      \det(A) = 12 - (-2) = 14
    </me>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>\mathcal{B} = \left\{ \mathbf{v_1} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix},\,
      \mathbf{v_2} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m>.
    </p>

   <image width="50%">
   <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="parallelogram-image-finalexam-review-2">
<diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
         dimensions="(300, 300)" margins="5">
  <definition>v1=(1,2)</definition>
  <definition>v2=(1,-2)</definition>
  <coordinates bbox="(-5,-5,5,5)">
    <grid spacings="((-6,2,6),(-6,2,6))" stroke="#ddd"/> 
    <grid basis="(v1,v2)" stroke="lightgray"/>
    <axes hticks="(-6,1,6)" vticks="(-6,1,6)"/>
    <vector v="v1" alignment="ne" stroke="blue">
      <m>{\mathbf v}_1</m>
    </vector>
    <vector v="v2" alignment="se" stroke="blue">
      <m>{\mathbf v}_2</m>
    </vector>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
  </prefigure>
</image>

    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li><p>Is <m>\mathcal{B}</m> a basis of <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>? Defend your answer.</p></li>
      <li><p>Find a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix that will convert <m>B</m>-coordinate vectors to standard coordinate vectors.  That is, find a matrix <m>P</m> with <m>\mathbf{y}=P\left\{ \mathbf{y}\right\}_{\mathcal{B}}</m> for every vector <m>\mathbf{y}</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Find a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix that will convert standard coordinate vectors to <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinate vectors.  That is, find a matrix <m>Q</m> with <m>\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{B}}=Q\left\{ \mathbf{y}\right\}</m> for every vector <m>\mathbf{y}</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>If <m>\{y\}_{\mathcal{B}} = \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>, find <m>y</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>If <m>y = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}</m>, find <m>\{y\}_{\mathcal{B}}</m>.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
  <solution>
  <ol>
  <li marker="(a)">
    <p>Yes, the vectors are linearly independent and span <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>. If they were the columns in a matrix <m>A</m>, the matrix in the RREF would be the <m>2 \times 2</m> identity matrix with a pivot in each row and in each column.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p><m>P = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}</m></p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p><m>Q = P^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} \tfrac{1}{2} \amp \tfrac{1}{4} \\ \tfrac{1}{2} \amp -\tfrac{1}{4} \end{bmatrix}</m></p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p><m>
      \mathbf{y} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -10 \end{bmatrix}
    </m></p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p><m>
      \{\mathbf{y}\}_{\mathcal{B}} =
      \begin{bmatrix} \tfrac{1}{2} \amp \tfrac{1}{4} \\ \tfrac{1}{2} \amp -\tfrac{1}{4} \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 3.25 \\ 0.75 \end{bmatrix}
    </m></p>
  </li>
</ol>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      The elementary matrices
      <m>E_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ -2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>
      and
      <m>E_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -\tfrac{4}{3} \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>
      transform the matrix
      <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 4 \amp 6 \\ 4 \amp 5 \amp 6 \\ 0 \amp -4 \amp -8 \end{bmatrix}</m>
      to
      <m>U = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 4 \amp 6 \\ 0 \amp -3 \amp -6 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
      That is, <m>E_2 E_1 A = U</m>.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><p>Find the LU-factorization of <m>A</m>.  That is, determine a lower triangular matrix <m>L</m> so that <m>A=LU</m> with <m>A</m> and <m>U</m> as given above.</p></li>
      <li><p>Is <m>A</m> invertible?  How can you decide without attempting to find the inverse of <m>A</m> through computation?</p></li>
      <li><p>Use the LU-factorization to solve <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m> for <m>\mathbf{b} = \begin{bmatrix} 14 \\ 19 \\ -12 \end{bmatrix}</m>.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li>
    <p><m>
      L = E_1^{-1} E_2^{-1}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp \tfrac{4}{3} \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp \tfrac{4}{3} \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
    </m></p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>No. <m>\det(U) = 0</m> since it has a 0 row and the product of its diagonal entries is 0. So det(A) is also 0 and hence not invertible.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      We solve <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m> as <m>LU\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m>. Set <m>\mathbf{y}=U\mathbf{x}</m>. Then <m>L\mathbf{y}=\mathbf{b}</m>.  That is, we solve the following via forward substitution:
      <me>
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix} y_1 \\ y_2 \\ y_3 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 14 \\ 19 \\ -12 \end{bmatrix}
      </me>
      This gives <m>\mathbf{y} = \begin{bmatrix} 14 \\ -9 \\ -21 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
      We then use back substitution to find <m>\mathbf{x}</m>:
      <me>
      \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 4 \amp 6 \\ 0 \amp -3 \amp -6 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 14 \\ -9 \\ -21 \end{bmatrix}
      </me>
      This gives <m>\mathbf{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 11.5 \\ -18 \\ 10.5 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      A <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix <m>A</m> has eigenvalues
      <m>\lambda_1=-2</m> with eigenvector <m>\mathbf{v}_1=\begin{bmatrix}3\\2\end{bmatrix}</m>
      and <m>\lambda_2=3</m> with eigenvector <m>\mathbf{v}_2=\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>.
      Find <m>A^4 \begin{bmatrix}5\\-1\end{bmatrix}</m>.  Hint: Do not try to find <m>A</m>.  The point is to do this without actually computing <m>A^4</m>.  Instead, write <m>\begin{bmatrix}5\\-1\end{bmatrix}</m> as a linear combination of <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m>.  
    </p>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    
<p>
      To write <m>\begin{bmatrix} 5 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}</m> as a linear combination of <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m>, we use an augmented matrix:
      <me>
      \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp 1 \amp 5 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}
      \sim
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 6 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -13 \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p> 
   <p>
      So,
      <me>\begin{bmatrix} 5 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} = 6 \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} - 13 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</me>    
which means that
      <md>
      <mrow> A^4 \begin{bmatrix} 5 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}
      \mathstrut \amp = A^4 \left( 6 \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} - 13 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\
     1 \end{bmatrix} \right) </mrow>
      <mrow> \mathstrut \amp = 6(-2)^4 \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} -13(3)^4 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} </mrow>
      <mrow> \mathstrut \amp = 96 \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} -1053 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} </mrow>
      <mrow> \mathstrut \amp = \begin{bmatrix} -765 \\ -861 \end{bmatrix}. </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>A=\begin{bmatrix}2 \amp -2 \\ -2 \amp 5\end{bmatrix}</m>.
      Find the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> and find a basis for each eigenspace.
      Then, identify a diagonal matrix <m>D</m> and an orthogonal matrix <m>P</m> such that <m>A=PDP^{-1}</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      <m>\det(A-\lambda I)=\lambda^2-7\lambda+6=(\lambda-6)(\lambda-1)</m>
      so that the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> are <m>\lambda_1=6</m> and <m>\lambda_2=1</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      A basis for the eigenspace <m>E_{\{6\}}</m> is
      <m>\left\{\begin{bmatrix}1\\-2\end{bmatrix}\right\}</m>
      and a basis for the eigenspace <m>E_{\{1\}}</m> is
      <m>\left\{\begin{bmatrix}2\\1\end{bmatrix}\right\}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      So we can diagonalize <m>A</m> using
      <m>D=\begin{bmatrix}6 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}</m>,
      and <m>P=\begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 2 \\ -2 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the matrix <m>A=\begin{bmatrix}6 \amp 8 \\ -1 \amp 2\end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><p>Find the characteristic polynomial of <m>A</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Find a (complex) eigenvector for <m>A</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Find an invertible matrix <m>P</m> and a matrix <m>C=\begin{bmatrix}a \amp -b \\ b \amp a\end{bmatrix}</m> such that <m>A=PCP^{-1}</m>.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
  <solution>
  <ol marker="(a)">
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>\det(A- \lambda I) = \begin{vmatrix} 6 - \lambda \amp 8 \\ -1 \amp 2 - \lambda \end{vmatrix} = \lambda^2 - 8\lambda + 20</m>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Using the quadratic formula to solve <m>\lambda^2 -8\lambda+20 =</m> gives <m>\lambda = 4 \pm 2i</m> (two complex eigenvalues).
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        For <m>\lambda = 4 + 2i</m>, we solve <m>(A- \lambda I)\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{0}</m> using an augmented matrix:
        <me>
        \begin{bmatrix} 2 - 2i \amp 8 \amp 0 \\ -1 \amp -2 - 2i \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
        \sim
        \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 + 2i \amp 0 \\ 2 - 2i \amp 8 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
        \sim
        \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 + 2i \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
        </me>
        so that an eigenvector of <m>A</m> is
        <me>\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 + 2i \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} - i \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</me>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Here <m>P = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp -2 \\ -1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> so that <m>P^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -1 \\ -\tfrac{1}{2} \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>C = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp -2 \\ 2 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      For the following, decide if the statement is True or False. If it is false, give a counterexample. If it is true, explain why.
    </p>
    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li><p>The sum of two eigenvectors of a matrix <m>A</m> is always an eigenvector of <m>A</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>The sum of two eigenvectors of a matrix <m>A</m> is never an eigenvector of <m>A</m>.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li>
        <p>
          False. Counterexample: <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Eigenvector for eigenvalue 1: <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Eigenvector for eigenvalue 2: <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Their sum is <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Check: <m>A \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          which is not a scalar multiple of <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          So the sum is not necessarily an eigenvector.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          False. Counterexample: <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Every nonzero vector is an eigenvector (eigenvalue 3).
          So if <m>\mathbf{u} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          <m>\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, then
          <m>\mathbf{u} + \mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>
          is also an eigenvector.
          In general, if two eigenvectors correspond to the same eigenvalue, any linear combination (including their sum) is an eigenvector.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the following subset of the vector space of all real-valued functions
      <me>S = \{\cos^2 t,\sin^2 t,\cos(2t),\sin(2t),1\}.</me>
      Let <m>W = \text{span } S</m>. What is <m>\dim W</m>?
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Since <m>\cos^2(t) = \frac{1 + \cos(2t)}{2}</m> and
      <m>\sin^2(t) = \frac{1 - \cos(2t)}{2}</m>, these two functions are linear combinations are <m>1</m> and <m>\cos(2t)</m>.
      So <m>W = \text{span}\{\cos(2t),\sin(2t),1\}</m>.
      These functions are all linearly independent so that <m>\dim W = 3</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Is the function <m>f(x) = x+5</m> in the subspace spanned by
      <m>g(x) = x+1</m> and <m>h(x) = x+3</m>? Explain.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Yes. In fact, <m>f(x) = -1g(x) + 2h(x)</m>.
      This is essentially the same as asking if the vector
      <m>\begin{bmatrix} 5 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>
      is in the subspace spanned by
      <m>\left\{\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}\right\}</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      In the following, decide if the set <m>S</m> is a basis, an orthogonal basis, an orthonormal basis, or not a basis for <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.
    </p>
    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li><p><m>S = \left\{\begin{bmatrix} 1/3 \\ 1/3 \\ 1/3 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} -1/2 \\ 0 \\ 1/2 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}\right\}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>S = \left\{\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 5 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}\right\}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><m>S = \left\{\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -4 \\ 7 \end{bmatrix}\right\}</m></p></li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li><p>Basis</p></li>
      <li><p>Not a Basis</p></li>
      <li><p>Orthogonal Basis</p></li>
    </ol>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Suppose a subspace <m>W</m> of <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m> is formed by
      <me>
      \mathbf{u}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},\;
      \mathbf{u}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li><p>Find an orthogonal basis for <m>W</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Find the matrix <m>P</m> that projects vectors orthogonally onto <m>W</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Find the closest point to <m>y = \begin{bmatrix} 10 \\ 10 \\ 18 \end{bmatrix}</m> in the subspace <m>U</m> of <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m> spanned by the orthogonal basis vectors <m>\mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Find the orthogonal projection of <m>\begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> onto <m>W</m>.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li>
        <p>
          Let <m>\mathbf{w}_1 = \mathbf{u}_1</m>. Let <m>\mathbf{w}_2 = \mathbf{u}_2 - \hat{\mathbf{u}}_2</m> where
          <m>\hat{\mathbf{u}}_2 = \frac{\mathbf{u}_2 \cdot \mathbf{u}_1}{\mathbf{u}_1 \cdot \mathbf{u}_1} \mathbf{u}_1 = \tfrac{1}{6} \mathbf{u}_1</m>.
          So an orthogonal basis for <m>W</m> is <m>\{\mathbf{w}_1,\mathbf{w}_2\}</m> where
          <m>\mathbf{w}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          <m>\mathbf{w}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} - \begin{bmatrix} -2/6 \\ 1/6 \\ 1/6 \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} 4/6 \\ -7/6 \\ -1/6 \end{bmatrix}.</m>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <m>P = QQ^T</m> where
          <m>
          Q = [\mathbf{w}_1 \mathbf{w}_2] =
          \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 4/6 \\ 1 \amp -7/6 \\ 1 \amp -1/6 \end{bmatrix}.
          </m>
          So
          <md>
          <mrow> P \mathstrut \amp =
          \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 4/6 \\ 1 \amp -7/6 \\ 1 \amp -1/6 \end{bmatrix}
          \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 4/6 \amp -7/6 \amp -1/6 \end{bmatrix} </mrow>
          <mrow> \mathstrut \amp =
          \begin{bmatrix} 40/9 \amp 11/9 \amp 17/9 \\ 11/9 \amp 85/36 \amp 43/36 \\ 17/9 \amp 43/36 \amp 37/36 \end{bmatrix}. </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The projection of <m>\mathbf{y}</m> onto <m>U</m> is given by
          <md>
            <mrow> \frac{\mathbf{y} \cdot \mathbf{v}_1}{\mathbf{v}_1 \cdot \mathbf{v}_1} \mathbf{v}_1 + \frac{\mathbf{y} \cdot \mathbf{v}_2}{\mathbf{v}_2 \cdot \mathbf{v}_2} \mathbf{v}_2
          \mathstrut \amp = \frac{30}{5} \mathbf{v}_1 + \frac{54}{9} \mathbf{v}_2 </mrow>
          <mrow> \mathstrut \amp = 6 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + 6 \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix} </mrow>
          <mrow> \mathstrut \amp = \begin{bmatrix} 6 \\ 12 \\ 18 \end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <me>
          \begin{bmatrix} 40/9 \amp 11/9 \amp 17/9 \\ 11/9 \amp 85/36 \amp 43/36 \\ 17/9 \amp 43/36 \amp 37/36 \end{bmatrix}
          \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
          =
          \begin{bmatrix} 137/9 \\ 175/36 \\ 241/36 \end{bmatrix}
          </me>;
          Note that
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 137/9 \\ 175/36 \\ 241/36 \end{bmatrix}</m>
          is a linear combination of <m>\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> since
          <me>
          \begin{bmatrix} 137/9 \\ 175/36 \\ 241/36 \end{bmatrix}
          = \tfrac{241}{36} \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
          + \tfrac{11}{6} \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Apply the Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization process to the following basis for a 3-dimensional subspace <m>W</m> of <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m>:
      <me>
      \left\{
      \mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
      \mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
      \mathbf{v}_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 5 \\ 4 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
      \right\}.
      </me>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Start by setting <m>\mathbf{u}_1 = \mathbf{v}_1</m>. Then, define
    </p>
    <p>
      <md>
      <mrow> \mathbf{u}_2 \mathstrut \amp = \mathbf{v}_2 - \frac{\mathbf{v}_2 \cdot \mathbf{u}_1}{\mathbf{u}_1 \cdot \mathbf{u}_1} \mathbf{u}_1 </mrow>
      <mrow> \mathstrut \amp =
      \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
      -
      \frac{6}{2}
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} </mrow>
      <mrow> \mathstrut \amp =
      \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}. </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Finally, define
    </p>
    <p>
      <md>
      <mrow> \mathbf{u}_3 \mathstrut \amp = \mathbf{v}_3
      - \frac{\mathbf{v}_3 \cdot \mathbf{u}_1}{\mathbf{u}_1 \cdot \mathbf{u}_1} \mathbf{u}_1
      - \frac{\mathbf{v}_3 \cdot \mathbf{u}_2}{\mathbf{u}_2 \cdot \mathbf{u}_2} \mathbf{u}_2 </mrow> 
      <mrow> \mathstrut \amp =
      \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 5 \\ 4 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
      -
      \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 4 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
      -
      \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 2 \\ 2 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} </mrow> 
      <mrow> \mathstrut \amp =
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}. </mrow> 
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      The orthogonal basis constructed is then
      <me>
      \left\{
      \mathbf{u}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
      \mathbf{u}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
      \mathbf{u}_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
      \right\}.
      </me>
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Show that the linear system
      <m>
      \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp 1 \\ 3 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix} 5 \\ 7 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}
      </m>
      is inconsistent. What value of
      <m>\begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}</m>
      comes closest to solving this system, in the sense that
      <me>
      \left|
      \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp 1 \\ 3 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}
      -
      \begin{bmatrix} 5 \\ 7 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}
      \right|^2
      </me>
      is minimized?
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      If <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp 1 \\ 3 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
      <m>\mathbf{b} = \begin{bmatrix} 5 \\ 7 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, the answer can be expressed as
      <me>\begin{bmatrix} \hat{\mathbf{x}} \\ \hat{\mathbf{y}} \end{bmatrix} = (A^T A)^{-1} A^T \mathbf{b} = \begin{bmatrix} 12 \\ 18.67 \end{bmatrix}.</me>
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

</exercises>

</section>
</handout>